WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.954
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level two.

2
00:00:06.254 --> 00:00:10.023
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.323 --> 00:00:16.761
Unit seven: Corporate Strategy. Today you will use participle clauses to link strategic actions.

4
00:00:17.061 --> 00:00:19.585
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.533 --> 00:00:23.557
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.417 --> 00:00:26.045
Competitive advantage.

7
00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:31.046
Competitive advantage means something that helps a company beat its rivals.

8
00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:34.369
Low costs gave the firm a competitive advantage.

9
00:00:34.983 --> 00:00:38.135
Your turn. Say: Competitive advantage.

10
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.663
Competitive advantage.

11
00:00:43.417 --> 00:00:44.638
Market positioning.

12
00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:49.890
Market positioning means how a company wants customers to see its brand.

13
00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:53.110
Careful market positioning attracted young buyers.

14
00:00:53.717 --> 00:00:56.764
Your turn. Say: Market positioning.

15
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:00.903
Market positioning.

16
00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:02.953
Growth strategy.

17
00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:08.121
Growth strategy means a plan for increasing sales and market share.

18
00:01:08.717 --> 00:01:11.482
The growth strategy focused on new countries.

19
00:01:12.083 --> 00:01:15.305
Your turn. Say: Growth strategy.

20
00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:19.632
Growth strategy.

21
00:01:20.383 --> 00:01:21.710
Diversification.

22
00:01:22.717 --> 00:01:27.503
Diversification means expanding into new products or markets to reduce risk.

23
00:01:28.117 --> 00:01:31.266
Diversification protected the company in a recession.

24
00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:34.781
Your turn. Say: Diversification.

25
00:01:37.633 --> 00:01:39.110
Diversification.

26
00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:40.874
Core business.

27
00:01:41.883 --> 00:01:45.833
Core business means the main activity from which a company earns most money.

28
00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:49.007
Rice milling is the firm's core business.

29
00:01:49.617 --> 00:01:52.559
Your turn. Say: Core business.

30
00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:56.749
Core business.

31
00:01:57.483 --> 00:01:58.833
Strategic plan.

32
00:01:59.817 --> 00:02:04.533
Strategic plan means a detailed plan for reaching long-term goals.

33
00:02:05.117 --> 00:02:07.430
The strategic plan covers five years.

34
00:02:08.017 --> 00:02:10.797
Your turn. Say: Strategic plan.

35
00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:15.122
Strategic plan.

36
00:02:15.883 --> 00:02:17.001
Business model.

37
00:02:18.017 --> 00:02:21.351
Business model means the way a company creates and earns money.

38
00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.960
A subscription business model brings steady income.

39
00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:28.458
Your turn. Say: Business model.

40
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:32.382
Business model.

41
00:02:33.117 --> 00:02:34.466
Long-term vision.

42
00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:40.155
Long-term vision means a clear idea of where a company wants to be in the future.

43
00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:43.504
A long-term vision guides every decision.

44
00:02:44.117 --> 00:02:47.721
Your turn. Say: Long-term vision.

45
00:02:50.567 --> 00:02:52.125
Long-term vision.

46
00:02:52.933 --> 00:02:56.909
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:05.441
Visal, a business report says 'Having expanded abroad, the firm now faces new competition.' What is 'Having expanded'?

48
00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:14.427
A perfect participle clause. It shows that one action happened before another, and both have the same subject.

49
00:03:15.517 --> 00:03:16.866
Are there other forms?

50
00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:29.405
Yes. 'Focusing on its core business, the company grew steadily' gives a reason or a time. 'Supported by a clear strategic plan, it succeeded' is passive.

51
00:03:30.517 --> 00:03:32.783
Why do strategy articles use them?

52
00:03:33.883 --> 00:03:43.522
They link actions concisely: building a competitive advantage, adopting diversification, and shaping a long-term vision.

53
00:03:44.667 --> 00:03:45.993
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:50.161
Grammar focus: Participle clauses in business writing.

55
00:03:50.461 --> 00:03:51.695
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:52.383 --> 00:03:56.136
Having expanded abroad, the firm now faces new competition.

57
00:03:57.050 --> 00:04:00.141
Focusing on its core business, the company grew steadily.

58
00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:04.779
Supported by a clear strategic plan, it reached its targets.

59
00:04:05.733 --> 00:04:16.292
The subject of the participle clause must be the same as the subject of the main clause. Use -ing (active), having + past participle (earlier action) or -ed (passive).

60
00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:21.455
Be careful! Don't say: Expanded abroad, the firm now faces it.

61
00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:27.025
Say: Having expanded abroad, the firm now faces it.

62
00:04:27.325 --> 00:04:30.358
Use having + participle for an earlier action.

63
00:04:31.167 --> 00:04:34.086
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:36.424
Strategy in Action.

65
00:04:36.724 --> 00:04:41.266
A successful company needs both a strong core business and a long-term vision.

66
00:04:42.267 --> 00:04:47.854
Having built a competitive advantage in its home market, the firm adopted a growth strategy abroad.

67
00:04:48.867 --> 00:04:54.094
Supported by a detailed strategic plan, its managers chose careful market positioning.

68
00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:59.456
Diversification followed, reducing the risk of relying on one product.

69
00:05:00.467 --> 00:05:04.196
A flexible business model keeps such a company strong over time.

70
00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:09.290
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:13.496
blank abroad, the firm now faces new competition.

72
00:05:13.796 --> 00:05:18.077
A, Having expanded. B, Expand. C, To have expanding.

73
00:05:21.633 --> 00:05:24.318
The answer is A: Having expanded.

74
00:05:25.483 --> 00:05:29.073
Something that helps a company beat its rivals is a competitive blank.

75
00:05:29.373 --> 00:05:32.888
A, model. B, advantage. C, positioning.

76
00:05:36.433 --> 00:05:38.328
The answer is B: advantage.

77
00:05:39.483 --> 00:05:42.690
Expanding into new products to reduce risk is blank.

78
00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:48.072
A, diversification. B, a core business. C, a strategic vision.

79
00:05:51.633 --> 00:05:54.132
The answer is A: diversification.

80
00:05:55.283 --> 00:05:57.027
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:57.327 --> 00:06:06.194
A, Focusing on costs, profits rose.. B, Focusing on costs, rose the profits it.. C, Focused on costs, profits rise it..

82
00:06:09.733 --> 00:06:13.834
The answer is A: Focusing on costs, profits rose..

83
00:06:14.983 --> 00:06:18.457
According to the reading, a flexible business model keeps a company…

84
00:06:18.757 --> 00:06:22.353
A, strong. B, small. C, secret.

85
00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:27.969
The answer is A: strong.

86
00:06:29.167 --> 00:06:32.063
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:32.917 --> 00:06:36.565
What makes a company's business model successful over the long term?

88
00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:46.723
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:47.023 --> 00:07:13.063
A successful business model solves a real problem for customers and earns money in a way that can last. It should also be flexible, because technology and customer needs change. Companies that have focused on quality, controlled costs and built loyal customers tend to survive. A clear long-term vision helps managers avoid short-term mistakes.

90
00:07:13.363 --> 00:07:15.049
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:15.950 --> 00:07:20.681
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and participle clauses in business writing.

92
00:07:20.981 --> 00:07:26.222
Today's value is strategic thinking. Think ahead, and choose your path with purpose.

93
00:07:26.522 --> 00:07:29.197
See you in Unit eight. Goodbye!

94
00:07:29.497 --> 00:07:32.010
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

